= Common ground =

== Overview ==
'''Common ground''' is information, assumptions, commitments, or other states treated as mutually available or shared among participants for purposes of communication.

It helps explain how speakers can leave information unstated, use pronouns and descriptions efficiently, coordinate references, and update a conversation.

== Not simply shared truth ==
Common ground is not the same as all information that two people happen to know.

Participants can share a belief without knowing that they share it.

Conversely, conversational participants can temporarily treat a proposition as accepted for the purpose of discussion without regarding it as objectively true.

Therefore:

<pre>
common ground ≠ objective truth
common ground ≠ all shared information
</pre>

== Mutuality ==
Many theories make some form of mutuality important.

A fact may be treated as common ground when each participant accepts it and recognizes that the others also accept it, perhaps under an idealized recursive model.

Real human interaction does not require explicitly computing an infinite tower of beliefs. Formal accounts use compact representations.

== Conversation updates ==
Assertions can propose additions to common ground.

Questions can establish issues to resolve.

Corrections, denials, challenges, and clarifications can block or revise proposed updates.

[[Presupposition|Presuppositions]] are often analyzed partly in terms of what is already common ground or can be accommodated into it.

== Common ground and context ==
[[Context (linguistics)|Context]] is broader.

It can include physical surroundings, time, place, linguistic co-text, social roles, and facts not mutually recognized.

Common ground is one structured component of communicative context.

== Competing theories ==
Accounts of common ground can emphasize:

* common belief;
* mutual knowledge;
* public commitments;
* accepted propositions;
* shared attention;
* joint action;
* normative conversational states.

The differences matter when modeling disagreement, deception, mistaken beliefs, and institutional discourse.

